<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.14.5: Identifying Key Features of Graphed Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01997</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.14.5: Identifying Key Features of Graphed Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>6ad2cf00-0ba3-4b8c-8110-e64da23933ca</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section id="sect-00001"><para id="para-00001">The equation \(y=(-16t−2)(t−1)\) represents the height in feet of a beach ball thrown by a child as a function of time, \(t\), in seconds.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
  <item>Since the equation is in factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at two points. Use this information to find the zeros of the function. Explain or show your reasoning.</item>
</list>
<section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00002"><title>Cool Down Activity</title><exercise id="_85f3be41-8f3f-4c2c-b8ea-ca5a6d375ec3" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00001"><para id="para-00002">Find one of the zeros of the functions using \((-16t−2)\).</para>
        </problem><solution id="sol-00001"><para id="para-00003">Correct! -0.125 or \(-\frac{1}{8}\). From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \(t=-0.125\) or \(-\frac{1}{8}\). The zero of the function is the same value.</para><para id="para-00004">The correct answer is -0.125 or \(-\frac{1}{8}\). From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at \(t=-0.125\) or \(-\frac{1}{8}\). The zero of the function is the same value.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<section id="sect-00003"><exercise id="_61432b7b-2103-4e11-9c7b-a2a0c07e1009" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00002"><para id="para-00005">Find one of the zeros of the functions using \((t−1)\).</para>
        </problem><solution id="sol-00002"><para id="para-00006">Correct! 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at t=1. The zero of the function is the same value.</para><para id="para-00007">The correct answer is 1. From the factored form, we can tell that the graph intersects the horizontal axis at t=1. The zero of the function is the same value.</para></solution></exercise></section><!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00001">
  <problem id="prob-00003">
    <para id="para-00008">What do the zeros tell us in this situation?</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00003">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00009">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00010">The zeros mean the time in seconds when the ball is on the ground.</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<section id="sect-00004"><exercise id="_0c87020e-f0c2-47f7-b473-acf8830dad35" class="multiple-choice"><problem id="prob-00004"><para id="para-00011">Are both zeros meaningful?</para>
      </problem><solution id="sol-00004"><para id="para-00012">Correct! No. Only one of them is meaningful. The negative zero doesn't mean anything here.</para><para id="para-00013">Incorrect. The correct answer is No. Only one of them is meaningful. The negative zero doesn't mean anything here.</para></solution></exercise></section>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise id="exer-00002">
  <problem id="prob-00005">
    <para id="para-00014">From what height, in feet, is the beach ball thrown? Explain or show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution id="sol-00005">
    
    
  
  <para id="para-00015">Compare your answer:</para>
    
    <para id="para-00016">2. The height when \(t=0\) is \((-16(0)−2)(0−1)=(-2)(-1)=2\). If you graph the function, the graph shows a \(y\)-intercepts of \((0,2)\).</para>
  
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End --></section>
